mentId": null,
      "startIndex": 3594,
      "endIndex": 4167,
      "namedStyleType": "NORMAL_TEXT",
      "isListItem": false,
      "listId": null,
      "nestingLevel": null,
      "table": null,
      "text": "Soup gives herbs a familiar household purpose. Lecture 5 describes Chinese medicine as a cultural framework shaping everyday food and health habits (GEC1044 Lecture 5, n.d.). Nourishing life (养生) and medicine and food sharing a source (药食同源) help explain how the display connects meals with health maintenance. Lim et al. (2005) identify health maintenance as an important reason for dietary herb use. Our fieldwork illustrates this through cooking: households encounter traditional ingredients as part of preparing familiar meals, rather than only when seeking treatment.",
      "protectedControls": []
    },
    {
      "paragraphId": "P00048",
      "path": "tabs.t.0.body.content[37]",
      "tabId": "t.0",
      "tabTitle": "Tab 1",
      "segmentId": null,
      "startIndex": 4167,
      "endIndex": 4204,
      "namedStyleType": "HEADING_2",
      "isListItem": false,
      "listId": null,
      "nestingLevel": null,
      "table": null,
      "text": "3.2 Soup packs and light nourishment",
      "protectedControls": []
    },
    {
      "paragraphId": "P00049",
      "path": "tabs.t.0.body.content[38]",
      "tabId": "t.0",
      "tabTitle": "Tab 1",
      "segmentId": null,
      "startIndex": 4204,
      "endIndex": 4733,
      "namedStyleType": "NORMAL_TEXT",
      "isListItem": false,
      "listId": null,
      "nestingLevel": null,
      "table": null,
      "text": "Table A2 records five soup labels: Qing Bu Liang (清补凉), Dang Shen–Huang Qi (党参北芪补气汤), Yu Zhu–Huai Shan (玉竹淮山润燥汤), goji–red date (枸杞红枣养生汤) and Five Finger Fig (五指毛桃健脾汤). Staff linked them with aiding digestion (助消化), moistening (润燥) and light nourishment (qing bu / 清补). Light nourishment offers an everyday dietary approach alongside stronger tonification (da bu / 大补). Pre-packaged ingredients translate these ideas into household practice: the shop selects and portions the materials, while buyers still cook the soup at home.",
      "protectedControls": []
    },
    {
      "paragraphId": "P00050",
      "path": "tabs.t.0.body.content[39]",
      "tabId": "t.0",
      "tabTitle": "Tab 1",
      "segmentId": null,
      "startIndex": 4733,
      "endIndex": 5144,
      "namedStyleType": "NORMAL_TEXT",
      "isListItem": false,
      "listId": null,
      "nestingLevel": null,
      "table": null,
      "text": "Several soups, especially Five Finger Fig, emphasise Spleen/digestive support. The TCM Spleen refers to a broader functional system, rather than the anatomical organ (WHO, 2022). However, the labels also describe moistening and general nourishment, so the range should not be reduced to one organ system. Their differing purposes show how similar cooking formats can communicate distinct traditional-use ideas.",
      "protectedControls": []
    },
    {
      "paragraphId": "P00051",
      "path": "tabs.t.0.body.content[40]",
      "tabId": "t.0",
      "tabTitle": "Tab 1",
      "segmentId": null,
      "startIndex": 5144,
      "endIndex": 5198,
      "namedStyleType": "HEADING_2",
      "isListItem": false,
      "listId": null,
      "nestingLevel": null,
      "table": null,
      "text": "3.3 Singapore weather and the appeal of lighter soups",
      "protectedControls": []
    },
    {
      "paragraphId": "P00052",
      "path": "tabs.t.0.body.content[41]",
      "tabId": "t.0",
      "tabTitle": "Tab 1",
      "segmentId": null,
      "startIndex": 5198,
      "endIndex": 5596,
      "namedStyleType": "NORMAL_TEXT",
      "isListItem": false,
      "listId": null,
      "nestingLevel": null,
      "table": null,
      "text": "Staff described monk fruit and lighter soups as popular, linking this perceived demand to Singapore’s environment. Meteorological Service Singapore describes high temperatures, heavy rainfall and humidity throughout the year, with monsoon and inter-monsoon periods (Meteorological Service Singapore [MSS], n.d.). This differs from the four temperate seasons underlying classical seasonal guidance.",
      "protectedControls": []
    },
    {
      "paragraphId": "P00053",
      "path": "tabs.t.0.body.content[42]",
      "tabId": "t.0",
      "tabTitle": "Tab 1",
      "segmentId": null,
      "startIndex": 5596,
      "endIndex": 6250,
      "namedStyleType": "NORMAL_TEXT",
      "isListItem": false,
      "listId": null,
      "nestingLevel": null,
      "table": null,
      "text": "The Suwen links health maintenance to adapting to seasonal cycles (Unschuld & Tessenow, 2011, pp. 45–57). In that framework, Yu Zhu is associated with autumn dryness (qiu zao / 秋燥): it nourishes Yin, moistens dryness and generates fluids, particularly for Lung and Stomach Yin. Singapore has no equivalent four-season cycle, so the shop’s explanations apply these ideas through local conditions. Yu Zhu appears in the recorded Yu Zhu–Huai Shan soup, which staff described through gentle moistening and fluid replenishment. This illustrates a change in the language and setting of use, without establishing that climate determines individual suitability.",
      "protectedControls": []
    },
    {
      "paragraphId": "P00054",
      "path": "tabs.t.0.body.content[43]",
      "tabId": "t.0",
      "tabTitle": "Tab 1",
      "segmentId": null,
      "startIndex": 6250,
      "endIndex": 6253,
      "namedStyleType": "NORMAL_TEXT",
      "isListItem": false,
      "listId": null,
      "nestingLevel": null,
      "table": null,
      "text": "[INLINE_OBJECT kix.6g44dukdnhug][INLINE_OBJECT kix.mdopn387j5ro]",
      "protectedControls": []
    },
    {
      "paragraphId": "P00055",
      "path": "tabs.t.0.body.content[44]",
      "tabId": "t.0",
      "tabTitle": "Tab 1",
      "segmentId": null,
      "startIndex": 6253,
      "endIndex": 6303,
      "namedStyleType": "HEADING_1",
      "isListItem": false,
      "listId": null,
      "nestingLevel": null,
      "table": null,
      "text": "4 Convenience and changing ways of using products",
      "protectedControls": []
    },
    {
      "paragraphId": "P00056",
      "path": "tabs.t.0.body.content[45]",
      "tabId": "t.0",
      "tabTitle": "Tab 1",
      "segmentId": null,
      "startIndex": 6303,
      "endIndex": 6345,
      "namedStyleType": "HEADING_2",
      "isListItem": false,
      "listId": null,
      "nestingLevel": null,
      "table": null,
      "text": "4.1 Easier preparation for younger buyers",
      "protectedControls": []
    },
    {
      "paragraphId": "P00057",
      "path": "tabs.t.0.body.content[46]",
      "tabId": "t.0",
      "tabTitle": "Tab 1",
      "segmentId": null,
      "startIndex": 6345,
      "endIndex": 6583,
      "namedStyleType": "NORMAL_TEXT",
      "isListItem": false,
      "listId": null,
      "nestingLevel": null,
      "table": null,
      "text": "Staff described younger customers preferring teas, supplements and simpler preparations over raw herbs. Table A3 lists eight product categories, including ginseng tonics, herbal teas, soup packs, mood-support products and medicated oils.",
      "protectedControls": []
    },
    {
      "paragraphId": "P00058",
      "path": "tabs.t.0.body.content[47]",
      "tabId": "t.0",
      "tabTitle": "Tab 1",
      "segmentId": null,
      "startIndex": 6583,
      "endIndex": 6956,
      "namedStyleType": "NORMAL_TEXT",
      "isListItem": false,
      "listId": null,
      "nestingLevel": null,
      "table": null,
      "text": "Preselected soup packs transfer selection and portioning to sellers while households retain cooking; capsules reduce preparation further. Staff explained ingredient uses, and a multi-buy promotion presented packs through a familiar supermarket offer. Convenience therefore changes how preparation work is shared, helping traditional materials fit younger buyers’ routines.",
      "protectedControls": []
    },
    {
      "paragraphId": "P00059",
      "path": "tabs.t.0.body.content[48]",
      "tabId": "t.0",
      "tabTitle": "Tab 1",
      "segmentId": null,
      "startIndex": 6956,
      "endIndex": 6989,
      "namedStyleType": "HEADING_2",
      "isListItem": false,
      "listId": null,
      "nestingLevel": null,
      "table": null,
      "text": "4.2 Products for daily pressures",
      "protectedControls": []
    },
    {
      "paragraphId": "P00060",
      "path": "tabs.t.0.body.content[49]",
      "tabId": "t.0",
      "tabTitle": "Tab 1",
      "segmentId": null,
      "startIndex": 6989,
      "endIndex": 7545,
      "namedStyleType": "NORMAL_TEXT",
      "isListItem": false,
      "listId": null,
      "nestingLevel": null,
      "table": null,
      "text": "Staff linked late nights, long work hours and prolonged phone use to demand for liver-care or mood-support products. Nature’s Green Mind Soothe Capsules use the language of soothing Liver-related constraint (疏肝解郁) and are listed by HSA as a Singapore-manufactured Chinese Proprietary Medicine (CPM) (Health Sciences Authority [HSA], 2025b). Such descriptions connect TCM terminology with familiar concerns about stress and mental well-being. These are retail associations, rather than clinical diagnoses or evidence that the products treat mental illness.",
      "protectedControls": []
    },
    {
      "paragraphId": "P00061",
      "path": "tabs.t.0.body.content[50]",
      "tabId": "t.0",
      "tabTitle": "Tab 1",
      "segmentId": null,
      "startIndex": 7545,
      "endIndex": 8106,
      "namedStyleType": "NORMAL_TEXT",
      "isListItem": false,
      "listId": null,
      "nestingLevel": null,
      "table": null,
      "text": "Kumari’s (2024) report on ADP’s 2023 survey found weekly work-related stress among 68% of Singapore workers, compared with 61% across surveyed Asia-Pacific markets. This gives broader context to the shop’s product language, although it does not measure Tong Keow customers’ reasons for purchase. Together with Section 4.1, the staff account suggests that lifestyle pressures and convenience help explain demand for packaged products: familiar concerns are expressed through traditional terms, while capsules offer a preparation format that fits daily routines.",
      "protectedControls": []
    },
    {
      "paragraphId": "P00062",
      "path": "tabs.t.0.body.content[51]",
      "tabId": "t.0",
      "tabTitle": "Tab 1",
      "segmentId": null,
      "startIndex": 8106,
      "endIndex": 8171,
      "namedStyleType": "HEADING_2",
      "isListItem": false,
      "listId": null,
      "nestingLevel": null,
      "table": null,
      "text": "4.3 Preparation effort and staff-described lower-demand products",
      "protectedControls": []
    },
    {
      "paragraphId": "P00063",
      "path": "tabs.t.0.body.content[52]",
      "tabId": "t.0",
      "tabTitle": "Tab 1",
      "segmentId": null,
      "startIndex": 8171,
      "endIndex": 8839,
      "namedStyleType": "NORMAL_TEXT",
      "isListItem": false,
      "listId": null,
      "nestingLevel": null,
      "table": null,
      "text": "Lower-demand examples included Ban Zhi Lian (半枝莲), specialist herbs requiring combinations and strong tonic formulas (大补方) (Table A4). Staff associated their lower appeal with unfamiliarity and preparation effort, and considered stronger tonics less suitable for Singapore’s hot, humid climate than lighter qing bu (清补) options. Slower-selling items were stocked in smaller quantities. This comparison helps explain the appeal of prepared packs: shoppers receive selected, portioned ingredients instead of assembling combinations themselves. It also suggests that staff guidance and preparation services matter alongside the ingredients’ traditional-use descriptions.",
      "protected